From fca938dffbffe424b970c5fda6b65033bc6c9a48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matthew Ahrens <matt@mahrens.org>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:53:51 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] ZIL replay of an indirect write leaks later writes in the
 same block

Consider three sequential writes to a file, A, B and C, where A is
logged WR_INDIRECT and C is to a different part of the same block in
the same txg, and then an fsync().  If we crash during the fsync(),
after we come back up we may see writes A and C but not B.  While
POSIX doesn't specify anything before the fsync() completes, ZFS
intends to preserve ordering in as many cases as possible.

The problem occurs because when ZIL replay applies a WR_INDIRECT
record, it restores the entire on-disk block, not just the range that
the user wrote.  The on-disk block holds whatever the dbuf contained
when the log was committed, including later writes in the same txg.
So after a crash, replay can bring back writes whose own log records
were never written, out of order with the rest of the log.  Also, a
whole-block write into a file's last, partial block grows the file to
the block boundary.

Whole-block replay was added (with TX_WRITE2) to recover the data of a
second indirect write to the same block in the same txg, whose
dmu_sync() returned EALREADY.  That record is a TX_WRITE2, with no
block pointer: its data is only in the block of the TX_WRITE that
caused the EALREADY.  Since lwbs have been filled outside
zl_issuer_lock (OpenZFS 2.2), that TX_WRITE can come later in the log,
in a later lwb.  The earlier lwb's commit waiters are signaled without
waiting for the later lwb, so a crash can also lose a write that
fsync() acknowledged.  zvol_get_data() dropped such records instead,
with the same problem.

The fix is to give every WR_INDIRECT record the block pointer of a
block that holds its data, then replay only the logged range.  Logs
written by older software are still replayed as before.

- dmu_sync() no longer returns EALREADY.  If another dmu_sync() of the
  same buffer is in progress, it queues on that write, which gives it
  its block pointer when it completes; until then, a child zio holds
  the caller's log block.  If the buffer was already written, it
  returns that write's block pointer.  The caller logs an ordinary
  indirect TX_WRITE, so several records can share one block pointer,
  and each record is durable by itself.
  Claim and free already skip duplicate block pointers; scrub and
  traversal now do too.  Traversal frees the log when a pool without
  async_destroy destroys a filesystem, and the skip moves there from
  zdb -b.  If a block clone replaced the buffer, dmu_sync() returns
  EIO, so the caller waits for the txg to sync: the clone's block
  pointer is for a block from an earlier txg, which the log can't
  claim.
- zfs_replay_write() writes only the record's range of the block.  A
  record that extends past its block can only come from a damaged log
  or very old software, and fails with EINVAL.  An old-style (all-zero)
  hole block pointer is replayed as zeros.
- Logs from older software can contain TX_WRITE2 records.  Before
  replay, zil_replay() scans the log and stops at the first TX_WRITE2.
  If it finds one, the log is replayed as before: whole blocks, with
  z_replay_eof and zfs_replay_write2().
- zvol replay keeps writing the whole block: older software's
  zvol_get_data() dropped the record on EALREADY, leaving its data
  only in the earlier record's block.  A block device makes no
  ordering promise between unflushed writes.

There is no on-disk format change or feature flag, and older software
replays the new logs correctly.

Add slog_replay_indirect_eof (the file growth) and
slog_replay_indirect_order (indirect writes sharing a block: a log cut
at each log block must replay a prefix of the writes, zdb -b must
count each shared block once, and destroying the filesystem before
replay must free it once), which both fail without the fix.
Add block_cloning_replay_indirect, which checks that a clone over an
indirectly logged write doesn't cut the log short, and
slog_indirect_shared_error, which checks that when a shared block
fails to be written, fsync falls back to a txg sync.

This is also illumos 18510.

